WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.020
JEN PERSON: Today we're talking
about Hybrid Work, Cloud Run

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.850
CPU, and Cloud Spanner.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.280
I'm Jen Person, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.990
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.390
Lots of folks around the
world are working in new ways

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.420
across a broad range of
locations and working hours.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.780
Workspace has added new features
to help you work effectively

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.650
with your teams.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.080
For example, Spaces
lets you collaborate

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.540
while integrating Calendar,
Drive, Docs, and more.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.320
The blog post has info
about new hardware,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.270
setting flexible
locations and hours,

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.100
and quickly connecting
with teammates.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.920
So check out the full details.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.290
Cloud Run gives you the
ability to run containers

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.540
in a serverless platform
and only charges

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.750
for CPU and memory used
during a request or event.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.670
But what if your
container workload

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.350
requires background processing?

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.110
Well, now you can
allocate always on CPU

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.000
to ensure your app
will have access

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.250
to the resources needed
while still giving you

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.470
the benefit of low ops.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.877
There's a lot of cool
stats around Cloud Spanner

00:00:58.877 --> 00:01:01.210
and how you can take advantage
of the strong consistency

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.660
and high availability.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.540
If you haven't had a
chance to try it out,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.060
now is the perfect time
with this walk-through.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.330
Follow along to deploy a
sample application on Cloud Run

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.660
and Spanner by using Terraform,
and then scale up Spanner.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.270
If you want even more,
check out the repository

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.440
of other Terraform examples.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.150
Are you a fan of
the command line,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.910
but wish you could
get better visibility?

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.210
Never fear, Katie is here
with a quick git extension--

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.440
Git Deploy.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.230
Katie breaks down how
git hooks and extensions

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.240
work, and demonstrates
Git Deploy to help

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.430
you see more details
from Cloud Build,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.820
by streaming the logs
all with one command.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.980
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.300
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.450
Any thoughts or ideas, leave
us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.460
We'll see you in the Cloud.

